 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Design
     2	
     3	## Inputs and outputs
     4	
     5	Input is `.mori` source in `moriarty-financial-agreement-source/6`. The frontend produces the
     6	existing AST, plus a refinement environment. The checker produces verification conditions. The
     7	solver produces a verdict per obligation. The compiler produces a Compact kernel and an
     8	elimination ledger. Each discharged obligation produces a receipt.
     9	
    10	## Layering decision
    11	
    12	Index parameters and refinement predicates stay two layers. A type is a base, a predicate and a
    13	multiplicity. Base equality stays syntactic and solver-free. Indices compute result types.
    14	Predicates only constrain. Refinements erase before lowering; indices do not. This keeps
    15	`static-semantics.md`'s existing rule "No subtyping or implicit casts" true for the base.
    16	
    17	## The fragment
    18	
    19	The authoritative constructor inventory is the `OPERANDS` table in
    20	`src/successor/financial-expression-v1.ts`, which holds 48 Core constructors. The file
    21	`spec/successor/expression-signatures.json` lists 40 and self-declares
    22	`"status": "proposed-not-registered"`. Do not use the JSON for fragment analysis.
    23	
    24	Classification over the authoritative table gives `QF_UFLIA`. No constructor is unconditionally
    25	nonlinear. The whole decidability boundary is `Mul`, `FloorDiv` and `CeilDiv`.
    26	
    27	Obligations use unbounded `Int` with explicit range side conditions, not bitvectors. This is a
    28	measured decision. On the same obligation from `loan-lifecycle.mori`, deterministic z3
    29	`rlimit-count` gives 4,081 for the Int encoding against 16,698,420 for the bitvector encoding.
    30	On the `accrue` obligation with one 128-bit `FloorDiv` the figures are 1,978 against
    31	242,607,369. At `UInt256` the bitvector encoding exceeds 600 seconds while the Int encoding
    32	stays at 1,978. The Int encoding also transcribes the runtime directly, which applies a `BigInt`
    33	operation and then `numericFits`.
    34	
    35	## Rounding
    36	
    37	Rounding refinements are directional, not equational. Exact conservation is false in this
    38	language. `spec/examples/loan.mori` discards a remainder of 27000/36500, which reduces to 54/73,
    39	and that file's own policy block records the discard. The provable law is that floor never
    40	credits more than the exact quotient.
    41	
    42	The repo already restricts divisors. `arithmeticType()` raises `TYPE_SCALE_DIVISOR` unless a
    43	`ScaledAmount` divisor is a `LitUInt` equal to `10^scale`. `ProRata` is the only irreducibly
    44	nonlinear path.
    45	
    46	## Trust boundaries
    47	
    48	Three boundaries are distinct and must not be merged.
    49	
    50	The solver is in the trusted computing base. G16 must name it. A verdict of `unknown` is a
    51	rejection, not a warning. Budgets are z3 `rlimit`, not wall-clock, so a verdict is deterministic.
    52	
    53	The prover is untrusted. Circuit parameters `before`, `remaining`, `revision` carry no binding
    54	to ledger state while MC02 through MC05 stay open. An assert whose subject reads a circuit
    55	parameter is input validation and is not deletable. Shrinking the assumption set retroactively
    56	breaks earlier deletions, so a deletion taken against an assumption that MC02 has not yet
    57	established is a live unsoundness.
    58	
    59	The ledger is the third boundary. Static linearity covers the intra-transaction half at no
    60	constraint cost. A nullifier mechanism must cover the cross-transaction half. MC04 requires
    61	unique predecessor consumption but does not require that the published nullifier set equals the
    62	set the checker recorded. Without that clause a prover publishes an empty set and satisfies MC04
    63	vacuously.
    64	
    65	## Semantic guarantees against backend feasibility
    66	
    67	The semantic guarantee is that a discharged obligation holds for every admitted input. That
    68	guarantee is independent of the backend.
    69	
    70	Proving cost is a separate claim and rests on a proxy. The cost unit is one bit of a
    71	`constrain_bits` or `less_than` width in retained ZKIR under
    72	`evidence/moriarty-completion-program-2026-09-07/MC01/compact-mapping/compiled/`. The model
    73	reproduces the four measured circuits exactly: loan 5540 and 3008, swap 6660 and 2208. If the
    74	Midnight backend uses lookup arguments for range checks, the proxy overweights them and every
    75	percentage moves. No Compact toolchain and no proving keys exist in this checkout;
    76	`keysGenerated` is false in retained evidence.
    77	
    78	Assert deletion is not where the cost is. One of the loan kernel's 19 asserts is deletable. The
    79	ten literal comparisons cost zero bit-constraints and are not deletable, because their subjects
    80	read `before`. The cost is in hint gadgets, which are 57.8% of `accrue`. One gadget,
    81	`checkedMul(e14, e15, hints.h2)` at `compact/generated/loan/kernel.compact:36`, multiplies the
    82	literals 100 and 365 through six witness limbs.
    83	
    84	## Failure handling
    85	
    86	A failed obligation produces a source-located diagnostic carrying a counterexample. An
    87	`unknown` verdict fails closed and blocks the corresponding gadget change. A stale receipt is
    88	rejected. A constraint budget may never be met by omitting an undischarged obligation; the
    89	build fails instead.
    90	
    91	## Dependencies
    92	
    93	SP02 `entryGates` requires `rp01-full`, which is open. Profile `/6` implementation is not
    94	admissible today. The design rows and the read-only advisory analyser are admissible now.
    95	
    96	No program is currently covered by both K and a Compact kernel. K runs `/5`
    97	`loan-lifecycle.mori`; the kernel compiles `/1` `loan.mori`. End-to-end checking of any
    98	deletion is blocked until one program is covered by both.

